% 47-self-hosting.weft — Stage 8b: the arena allocator, written in C,
% compiled by kcc itself, and linked back into the compiler.

\chapter{Self-Hosting: The Arena in C}
\label{ch:self-hosting-arena}

Every preceding chapter builds \kcc{} out of hand-written ARM64
assembly.  This chapter closes a loop: it re-implements one of the
compiler's own subsystems---the arena allocator of
Chapter~\ref{ch:arena}---in C, compiles that C with \kcc{}, and links
the resulting object back into the compiler in place of the assembly
version.  If the twice-removed compiler (assembly \kcc{} compiling C
that becomes part of \kcc{}) still passes the entire code-generation
suite, then \kcc{} has demonstrably compiled a non-trivial, correctness-%
critical piece of itself.  This is the first concrete step of
Stage~8b.

The arena is the ideal candidate.  It is small, self-contained, and
exercised by \emph{every} other subsystem: tokens, AST nodes, types,
symbols, IR, and machine code all draw their storage from it.  A single
mistake in the C port---a mis-computed offset, a botched pointer
comparison, an off-by-one in the alignment---would corrupt allocations
and cascade into failures across the whole suite.  Passing all 201
tests is therefore a strong signal that the features this port relies
on (\texttt{struct} layout, \texttt{->} access, pointer casts and
arithmetic, external calls, globals, the ternary and bitwise operators)
are correct together, not merely in isolation.

\section{Interface and Chunk Layout}

The C port must be a drop-in replacement, so it exports exactly the
symbols the assembly arena did---\texttt{kcc\_arena\_init},
\texttt{kcc\_arena\_alloc}, and the global \texttt{kcc\_arena\_current}---%
and it depends only on the two syscall wrappers the assembly arena
already used.  Those wrappers stay in assembly; the port calls them as
ordinary \texttt{extern} functions, and the linker resolves the
references across objects exactly as \S\ref{sec:preregister-globals}
describes.

The \texttt{Chunk} structure mirrors the 24-byte header of
Chapter~\ref{ch:arena} field for field: \texttt{next} at offset~0,
\texttt{limit} at~8, \texttt{avail} at~16.  In the all-spill model each
struct field occupies one eight-byte slot, so this C layout and the
assembly layout coincide byte for byte---which is what lets the port
read and write the same header a future chunk allocation will expect.
\texttt{limit} and \texttt{avail} are \texttt{char~*} so that pointer
arithmetic on them counts bytes rather than being scaled.

@l c
@d Selfhost: arena preamble @{
struct Chunk {
    struct Chunk *next;
    char *limit;
    char *avail;
};

void *kcc_sys_mmap(void *addr, long len, int prot, int flags, int fd, long off);
void  kcc_sys_exit(int status);

struct Chunk *kcc_arena_current;
@}

\section{Initialization}

\texttt{kcc\_arena\_init} requests a 64\,KiB private, anonymous mapping
from the kernel (\texttt{prot}~=~\texttt{PROT\_READ|PROT\_WRITE}~=~3,
\texttt{flags}~=~\texttt{MAP\_PRIVATE|MAP\_ANON}~=~\texttt{0x1002}~=~4098),
bails out with status~1 on failure, and writes the three header fields:
an empty \texttt{next}, a \texttt{limit} one past the mapping, and an
\texttt{avail} pointing just past the 24-byte header.  The finished
chunk becomes the current one.  The failure test casts the returned
pointer to \texttt{long} and compares against $-1$
(\texttt{MAP\_FAILED}), mirroring the assembly's \texttt{cmn} check.

@l c
@d Selfhost: arena init @{

void kcc_arena_init(void) {
    struct Chunk *c = (struct Chunk *)kcc_sys_mmap(0, 65536, 3, 4098, -1, 0);
    if ((long)c == -1) kcc_sys_exit(1);
    c->next = 0;
    c->limit = (char *)c + 65536;
    c->avail = (char *)c + 24;
    kcc_arena_current = c;
}
@}

\section{Allocation}

\texttt{kcc\_arena\_alloc} first rounds the request up to the next
multiple of eight with the classic \texttt{(size + 7) \& \textasciitilde 7}.
The fast path is a single bounds check: if the bumped \texttt{avail}
would still fit under \texttt{limit}, it advances the pointer and
returns the old value.  Both operands of the comparison are
\texttt{char~*}, so \texttt{p + size} is a true byte offset.

When the current chunk cannot satisfy the request, the slow path maps a
fresh chunk sized to the larger of the default 64\,KiB and the request
plus a header, links it ahead of the current chunk, and serves the
allocation from it.  Unlike the assembly version---which conservatively
set every chunk's \texttt{limit} to base~+~64\,KiB---this port sets
\texttt{limit} to base~+~\emph{actual size}, so an oversized chunk is
fully usable; for every allocation the test suite makes, the two behave
identically.

@l c
@d Selfhost: arena alloc @{

void *kcc_arena_alloc(long size) {
    size = (size + 7) & ~7;
    struct Chunk *c = kcc_arena_current;
    char *p = c->avail;
    if (p + size <= c->limit) {
        c->avail = p + size;
        return p;
    }
    long need = size + 24;
    long chunksz = need > 65536 ? need : 65536;
    struct Chunk *n = (struct Chunk *)kcc_sys_mmap(0, chunksz, 3, 4098, -1, 0);
    if ((long)n == -1) kcc_sys_exit(2);
    n->next = c;
    n->limit = (char *)n + chunksz;
    char *base = (char *)n + 24;
    n->avail = base + size;
    kcc_arena_current = n;
    return base;
}
@}

\section{Assembling the Translation Unit}

The three fragments compose into one C translation unit, routed by the
assembly file to \texttt{tmp/selfhost/arena.c}.  This is the file
\texttt{make selfhost} feeds to \kcc{}.

@l c
@d Selfhost: arena @{
@<Selfhost: arena preamble@>
@<Selfhost: arena init@>
@<Selfhost: arena alloc@>
@}

\section{Verification}
\label{sec:selfhost-verify}

The \texttt{make selfhost} recipe performs the whole loop.  It compiles
\texttt{tmp/selfhost/arena.c} with the assembly-built \kcc{}
(\texttt{kcc -c arena.c -o arena\_c.o}), then relinks a second compiler
binary, \texttt{tmp/kcc\_selfhost}, from all the usual objects but with
the hand-written \texttt{arena.o} swapped out for the freshly compiled
\texttt{arena\_c.o}.  Finally it points the code-generation suite at
that binary.

The passing criterion is unforgiving and complete: \texttt{kcc\_selfhost}
must reproduce all 201 results of the assembly-built compiler.  Because
the arena underlies every allocation, and because the 201 programs
between them allocate enough to spill past a single 64\,KiB chunk many
times over, a green run means the C arena---compiled by \kcc{}---is
byte-for-byte behaviourally equivalent to the assembly original, slow
path and all.

@d Selfhost: verify @{
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# make selfhost — compile kcc's arena in C with kcc, relink, run the suite.
set -euo pipefail

SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path)
SH=tmp/selfhost
sources="main syscalls arena strings io hashtab vec strtab source lexer \
lexer_tables diag preprocessor parser expressions statements declarations \
types symtab conversions sema ir opt emit_arm64 linker codegen emit_macho dwarf driver"

echo "[1/3] compiling tmp/selfhost/arena.c with kcc"
tmp/kcc -c "$SH/arena.c" -o "$SH/arena_c.o" >/dev/null

echo "[2/3] linking tmp/kcc_selfhost (arena.o -> kcc-compiled arena_c.o)"
OBJ=""
for f in $sources; do
    if [ "$f" = "arena" ]; then OBJ="$OBJ $SH/arena_c.o"; else OBJ="$OBJ tmp/src/${f}.o"; fi
done
ld -o tmp/kcc_selfhost $OBJ -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64

echo "[3/3] running the codegen suite with the self-hosted arena"
cp tmp/kcc "$SH/kcc_asm_backup"
cp tmp/kcc_selfhost tmp/kcc
set +e
bash tmp/tests/test_codegen.sh
status=$?
cp "$SH/kcc_asm_backup" tmp/kcc
exit $status
@}
